.docname {Drawing and stamps}
.include {docs}

You edit the board with four tools, chosen in the toolbar or with .keybinding {1}, .keybinding {2}, .keybinding {3}, and .keybinding {4}: **Draw**, **Erase**, **Stamp**, and **Select**. The digit keys work on layouts that need Shift for digits, such as AZERTY, too.

## Draw and erase

With **Draw** selected, drag with the left mouse button to bring cells to life. With **Erase** selected, the same drag kills cells. The right mouse button always erases, whichever tool is selected.

Cells you drag across are changed one at a time, but the whole drag counts as a single edit for [undo](#undo-and-redo).

You can also edit without a mouse: press .keybinding {Tab} until the board is outlined, move the cell cursor with the arrow keys, and press .keybinding {Enter} to draw, erase, stamp, or select at it. Each press is a single edit. See [Editing the board from the keyboard](accessibility.qd#editing-the-board-from-the-keyboard).

## Stamps

A stamp places a whole pattern with one click.

1. Click **Stamp** or press .keybinding {3} to open the pattern palette, then click a pattern's thumbnail.
2. Move the mouse over the board. A blue *ghost*, outlined with the stamp's footprint, shows exactly which cells will be placed. It has its own colour so it never passes for cells already on the board.
3. Click to place the pattern. The stamp stays selected, so you can keep clicking to place copies.

.keybinding {C} drops the stamp, goes back to the Draw tool, and closes the palette. Clicking **Stamp**, or pressing .keybinding {3}, with no pattern selected picks the first one in the library. Switching to Draw or Erase closes the palette and hides the ghost but keeps the stamp selected, so clicking **Stamp** again brings both back.

The palette covers the left of the canvas. To reach the cells under it, pan the board, or press .keybinding {H} to hide the palette while you keep stamping. .keybinding {H} or **Stamp** brings it back.

You can add your own patterns, either for the current session through [RLE import](patterns.qd#importing-a-pattern) or permanently as [built-in patterns](patterns.qd#built-in-patterns).

### Rotating and flipping

| Key               | Button     | Effect                                   |
|-------------------|------------|------------------------------------------|
| .keybinding {Z}   | **Rotate** | Rotate the stamp a quarter turn clockwise |
| .keybinding {F}   | **Flip**   | Mirror the stamp horizontally            |

The stamp preview in the palette and the ghost on the board both show the orientation that will be placed. The keys also work while the palette is hidden.

## Selections

With **Select**, drag across the board to select a rectangle of cells. It is outlined in the same colour as a stamp's footprint. On a finite board, dragging past an edge stops the selection at it. Click without dragging to let go of the selection, or press .keybinding {Esc}.

| Key                               | Effect                                                   |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| .keybinding {Mod+A}               | Select the box around every live cell, and pick Select   |
| .keybinding {Mod+C}               | Copy the selection to the clipboard as RLE               |
| .keybinding {Mod+X}               | Copy the selection as RLE, then clear it                 |
| .keybinding {Mod+V}               | Paste RLE from the clipboard as the stamp                |
| .keybinding {Delete} or .keybinding {Backspace} | Kill every cell in the selection           |
| .keybinding {Esc}                 | Deselect                                                 |

Copied RLE keeps the selection's size, empty margins included, and carries the board's rule. Clearing and cutting are edits, so you can undo them. Switching to another tool, or between a finite and an unbounded board, drops the selection.

### Pasting

.keybinding {Mod+V} turns the RLE on the clipboard into the stamp and picks the **Stamp** tool, so a copied selection, or a pattern copied from another program, follows the pointer as a ghost until you click to place it. Each click places another copy, and .keybinding {Z} and .keybinding {F} rotate and flip it as with any stamp. A paste starts in the orientation it was copied in.

A pasted stamp is not added to the pattern library and is not saved in workspaces; choosing a library pattern or pressing .keybinding {C} puts it away. The board keeps its rule, whatever rule the pasted RLE names. To keep a pattern, [import it](patterns.qd#importing-a-pattern) instead.

## Board actions

| Action        | Key               | Effect                                                     |
|---------------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Randomize** | .keybinding {R}   | Replace the board using the density and seed chosen in Settings |
| **Clear**     | .keybinding {X}   | Kill every cell                                            |

Both are edits, so you can undo them. Randomize keeps the rule, edges, and generation
counter. In Settings, **Randomization** lets you choose a whole-number density from
0–100% (25% by default), and an optional seed. Leave the seed blank for a fresh board
each time; the same seed, density, and dimensions repeat the same cell layout.
The density slider applies as it moves and the seed applies with **Apply**; **Randomize now** also replaces the board.
An unbounded board has too many cells to replace, so Randomize fills the view and leaves everything else as it is. That stops at 2048 × 2048 cells in the middle of the view, and the notice says how much it filled.
Density is a probability for each cell, so the population is approximate. At 0% the
board is empty; at 100% every cell is alive.

## Undo and redo

| Key                                              | Effect                         |
|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| .keybinding {U} or .keybinding {Mod+Z}           | Undo the last edit and pause   |
| .keybinding {Mod+Shift+Z}                        | Redo the last undone edit      |

The toolbar has **Undo** and **Redo** buttons as well, shown as arrows curving left and right.

These are edits, and can be undone:

- drawing and erasing (one edit per drag)
- placing a stamp
- clearing or cutting a selection
- randomizing and clearing the board
- [resetting to start](simulation.qd#reset-to-start)
- changing the rule, including an import that changes the rule

Undo restores the board, the generation counter, and the rule as they were before the edit, and pauses playback so you can see the result. Redo reapplies an undone edit and also leaves playback paused. Making a new edit discards anything left to redo.

### History limits

History keeps up to 32 edits, within a budget of about 16 MiB of board snapshots. A snapshot takes one bit per cell, so every board size keeps all 32. On an unbounded board a snapshot grows with the pattern, so a very large one fits fewer edits, but at least one is always kept.

Resizing the board keeps the undo and redo history. Undoing past a resize keeps the board at its new size and brings back the cells of the earlier board that fit in it.

## Save an image

To save a picture of the board, open **Export** and click **Save image…**; the web version downloads it instead, as **Download image**. It saves a PNG of the [selection](#selections) if there is one, or else of the whole board, or on an unbounded board of the box around its live cells. The image is drawn from the cells rather than the screen, in the theme's live and dead colours, without the grid or cell age, and doesn't depend on the window or the zoom.

Each cell is a square of up to 8 pixels, as many as keep the image within 4096 pixels on its longer side, and a single pixel on anything larger. An image can be at most 16,384 cells on a side; for anything larger, select part of it. The file takes a bit a pixel, so even a 2048 × 2048 board saves small. As with workspaces, the file is written through a temporary one beside it, and the system file dialog asks before replacing an existing file.

## Record a GIF

To record the board as it runs, open **Export**, set **Generations**, and click **Save GIF…**. It records the same area as an image, from the current generation on, as a GIF that loops forever: 100 generations by default, and up to 1000. Recording steps a copy of the board, so the board itself plays on, and the dialog counts the generations recorded meanwhile. Another file can't be saved or opened until it finishes. **Cancel recording**, which shows while one is under way, stops it and saves nothing.

For a GIF that loops without a jump, click **Save one period…** instead. It records from the current generation until the board comes back to it, up to 1000 generations, so the last frame leads straight back into the first. The board has to be repeating already: a pattern still settling never comes back to where it is, and the GIF isn't saved. Run the board until the status bar shows its period first. The period is the whole board's, so a selection loops after the board's period even if what's inside it repeats sooner.

The web version downloads the GIF instead, as **Download GIF** and **Download one period**. It has one thread, so it records between frames, about half of each, and the board runs slower until the recording finishes.

Each generation shows for as long as it does at the playback speed. At ∞ it shows for 20 ms, 50 generations a second, the fastest that browsers play a GIF: they slow anything quicker to 10 frames a second. GIF times count hundredths of a second, so at 40 generations a second frames alternate between 30 and 20 ms.

Each cell is a square of up to 8 pixels, as many as keep the GIF within 1024 pixels on its longer side, and a pixel each on anything larger. A GIF can be at most 2048 cells on a side. Each frame after the first holds only the box around the cells whose colour changed, and a generation that changes nothing adds to the time the frame before it shows, so a lone glider on a large board records small. On an unbounded board the area is the box around the live cells when recording starts, and a ship that leaves it leaves the picture. If the board reaches its memory limit, the recording stops there. Like an image, a GIF has no grid.

With [cell age](interface.qd#cell-age) on, a GIF colours cells by age as the board does: newborn cells bright, old ones deeper, and trails fading where cells died. Ages count from the start of the recording, as after loading a board: cells already alive start out in the plain live colour, and empty cells with no trail. One period goes round the loop until every colour has settled into it, up to 60 generations, before it records, so the colours loop without a jump too. Ageing cells change colour every generation, so a GIF with cell age on is larger.

## Save and reopen workspaces

.keybinding {Mod+S} saves and .keybinding {Mod+O} opens, from the board or with **Import**, **Export**, or **Settings** open; results show in the dialog, or as a brief notice over the board. Reading and writing happen in the background, so the board keeps running while a large workspace saves or loads; until it finishes, another save or open is refused with a notice.

To save, open **Export** and click **Save workspace…**. It opens the
system file dialog in your Documents folder, or wherever you last saved or opened a
workspace. The dialog asks before replacing an existing file. The file is written
through a temporary one beside it, so a failed save leaves the old file as it was.
On Linux the file dialog comes from xdg-desktop-portal, so a portal backend must be
installed; see [Installation](install.qd#linux-x86_64).

To open one, open **Import** and click **Open workspace…**. It lets you choose a `.golw` file and checks it. If it is valid, you're
asked whether to replace the current board and imported pattern library. **Cancel**
(or Escape) keeps your current work. Files preserve board dimensions, exact cell positions, generation,
rule, boundary mode, playback speed, randomization density and seed, and imported pattern names, cells, and rules.
Replacing pauses playback, clears undo/redo, and turns Fill canvas off. Invalid files
are reported without asking, and leave the current workspace intact.

An unbounded board is saved with its cells where they are, and the size it returns to. It opens with its live cells in view, and builds from before unbounded boards can't read that file. Finite boards are saved as they always were.

Files are versioned JSON containing RLE data. Limits are 32 MiB per file, 256 imported
patterns, 1024 bytes per pattern name, and 4,194,304 live cells across the imported
patterns. A finite board doesn't count toward the cell limit; an unbounded one is limited to 67,108,864 live cells, though at 32 MiB a file runs out sooner unless they are in long runs. Camera position,
active stamp, cell-age trails, and undo history are not saved. Display settings remain
in the separate settings file. Saving is explicit; there is no automatic recovery.
